Now, `start+length` is the byte right after the segment. If the segment extends to the null terminator, `start+length` = null terminator. If not, `start+length` is the next character in the string.

For the OOB to happen, `s[2]` must read past the null terminator. This requires `start+length+1 > size`, i.e., `start+length >= size`. Since `start+length <= size`, this requires `start+length == size`, i.e., the segment extends to the null terminator.

If the segment extends to the null terminator and `s = start+length-1 = size-1`, then `s[1]` = null (not hex), so `s[2]` not read. No OOB.

